The program races four clients against a server whose accept cap is two, and
which of the two over-cap connections gets a 503 rather than a TCP reset is the
kernel's choice, not the program's. When the listen backlog drops the connection
first, the client prints `client N err 500 reading the response head: Connection
reset by peer` and `refusedSeen` comes back false instead of true.

Measured rather than assumed: 25 runs with eight busy cores alongside produced
the reset form twice, once for one client and once for two. It is also why
`npm test` failed intermittently before this marker existed - the suite runs in
parallel with src/e2e.test.js, which is load enough.

The cap accounting itself is stable (`peak=2`, `rejected=2`,
`accepted+rejected=4`, `drained=true` in every run); it is only the REASON the
over-cap client failed that moves.
